{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And one goat for a sin-offering.</b> חַטָּת is written defective, missing an אָלֶ״ף. And there is another anomaly here: in every other place where a sin-offering and a burnt-offering are brought, the sin-offering precedes the burnt-offering, but here the burnt-offering precedes the sin-offering — is this not a matter [worth investigating]? And I say: since this verse speaks of the sin of idolatry, as it says: ״וְכִי תִשְׁגּוּ וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ אֵת כָּל הַמִּצְווֹת״ — this is idolatry, for whoever acknowledges it is as one who denies the whole Torah. And in every transgression a person sins in thought and in deed, for every deed is preceded by thought, for if one acts without any intention at all, this is not called inadvertent (שׁוֹגֵג) but coerced (אוֹנֵס), and therefore every sinner needs to bring two atonements: the burnt-offering for the stirring of the heart, and the sin-offering for the sin of the deed. And even though every thought precedes the deed, nonetheless the deed is weightier than the thought, for the Holy One, blessed be He, never punishes for thought alone if it did not issue into action, as it is written (Psalms 66:18): ״אָוֶן אִם רָאִיתִי בְלִבִּי לֹא יִשְׁמַע ה׳״. Even though for the deed as well He does not punish when it is done without any thought or intention at all, nonetheless the deed is the essence of the sin; therefore one brings the sin-offering first, to remove the grave offense first, and afterward the burnt-offering to remove the offense of the thought — except in the case of idolatry, of which it is written (Ezekiel 14:5): ״לְמַעַן תְּפֹשׂ אֶת בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּלִבָּם״, because in idolatry the thought is the essence of the sin, for this is a matter dependent on the faith of the heart, and all the deeds — such as incense-offering, slaughter, libation, and prostration — are all subordinate to the thought. And thus the opening of the Sefer Mitzvot Gadol (SMaG) reads: not to entertain the thought that there is any other god, etc. Therefore he places the burnt-offering before the sin-offering, in order to remove the greater sin first — and that is the thought, upon which the burnt-offering comes, as it is written (Ezekiel 20:32): ״וְהָעוֹלָה עַל רוּחֲכֶם״ — and afterward the lesser one, which is the deed. And therefore ״לְחַטָּת״ is written defective, missing an אָלֶ״ף, for the אָלֶ״ף is the first letter and its number is one, indicating everywhere the oneness of the First Cause for all created beings, blessed be He; and this one who sins in idolatry denies His oneness, blessed be He, and there is no god in his designs, and therefore the אָלֶ״ף is missing in חַטָּת, to say that the absence of the אָלֶ״ף is precisely his sin, for the verse speaks of idolatry. And in this matter YHWH is distinguished from all His creations, for all of them act in two — in thought and in deed — but concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, it is said (Job 23:13): ״וְהוּא בְאֶחָד וּמִי יְשִׁיבֶנּוּ וְנַפְשׁוֹ אִוְּתָה וַיָּעַשׂ״, for immediately, as soon as YHWH thinks of some thing to make it, it is at once made — as soon as His soul desired and He thought of it to make it."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:30.445095136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}